{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for deployment note round 7 at exact clean head fa0b1881f11c16fe8b4abf4950ab554cb4a2c1e3.\n\nReviewed request git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:26302710125833f9cb0f77fba62aeec07fb113c7, artifact git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c86c39b2b526a4419d39a5fdb2a0092b41f98829, and prior report git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:24f4c3eaff2c052823d3cc7fc62236004098881c. Full make vet test race build and git diff --check pass. The parent topology, singleton claim name, absent-value lease, and recorded-E recovery direction are materially better. A concrete local receive-pack probe at /tmp/gitseq-deployment-r7.cVh02s confirms that one atomic push can create a claim, delete an inbox branch, create/update refs/notes/gitseq-batches, and carry a no-op refs/seq refspec; a second absent-value claimant is rejected and its inbox deletion is atomically rejected.\n\nFive blockers remain.\n\n1. The retained batch note preserves identifiers but not the objects recovery needs. In the same probe, after the live claim was deliberately deleted and reflogs expired, refs/notes/gitseq-batches still existed, but git gc --prune=now removed both recorded E and the candidate (cat-file exit 128 for each). A notes tree path/content is not commit-parent reachability. Therefore “recover from the candidates the note records” is impossible after exactly the claim-loss/GC state this repair targets. The retained batch object itself must keep E and candidates reachable, for example through durable parent topology under a retained protected ref; re-probe deletion plus GC.\n\n2. A --force-with-lease option does not assert an unrelated ref. With remote refs/seq/j advanced beyond observed P, an atomic claim+inbox-delete push carrying --force-with-lease=refs/seq/j:P but no refs/seq/j refspec succeeded, created the claim, and deleted the inbox. Git documents that the lease protects the ref only if it is going to be updated. Adding the explicit no-op P:refs/seq/j refspec made the same stale state reject every atomic update. The protocol and spike must state and exercise that refspec, not merely say the push “carries a lease.”\n\n3. The claimed protection for refs/notes/gitseq-batches has no stated GitHub mechanism. Current GitHub rulesets target branches or tags; push rulesets restrict file properties, not arbitrary ref namespaces. Thus case 7’s “deleting or rewriting refs/notes” refusal can currently pass only if an unproven hosted behavior supplies the missing ACL. Either prove that exact GitHub push and authorization behavior before presenting tier 1, or put the retained batch record under a ref namespace the documented controls can actually protect. The successful local atomic mixed-ref probe does not settle GitHub’s ref ACL.\n\n4. The state table still does not close. It omits the accounted/unaccounted batch-record dimension that now changes the no-claim verdict; “E/E/none = proceed” is false for an unaccounted note, and E is not even defined without a live claim. A no-claim divergent published/mirror tuple reaches no table row, only later prose. Put note state into the table and include explicit no-claim equal-accounted, equal-unaccounted, and divergent rows; reserve the catch-all for every remaining tuple.\n\n5. Spike case 7 lacks a same-credential positive control. All six expected refusals pass with a dead token or a principal that cannot write anything, so the case can succeed without exercising ref isolation. First prove that the exact submitter credential can create an allowed ordinary inbox ref, then run the six forbidden operations with that unchanged credential and verify durable refs remain byte-identical.\n\nThe cannot-fail audit finds cases 1, 2a/2b, 3-6, 8-10 otherwise capable of failing for their named subject at this head, subject to the table/object blockers above. Approval remains limited to an unchanged exact head; this head is not approved.","body":{"head":"fa0b1881f11c16fe8b4abf4950ab554cb4a2c1e3","probe":"/tmp/gitseq-deployment-r7.cVh02s","verdict":"changes-requested"}}